 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "4nlm5igYDAAJ",
   "from": "Gmail Marcin",
   "author": "80f00f6e5c28f957",
   "date": "2017-03-30T09:35:43Z",
   "subject": "methylkit/eDMR",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, I was looking at Klein and Hebestreit paper 20016 where methylKit in eDMR mode was compared to other software where regional significance are of interest.  I see that methylkit does not perform that great when it comes to the sensitivity methylkit/eDMR  (Fig3). I wanted to use this mode for differential analysis of the regions.  I am not sure if the reason for it is that maybe overdisperssion was not implemented yet before running the diff tests? I also did not like their description about the methylkit/eDMR as what comes into the analysis is already diff object.  Heterogeneity of the samples is taken in test already ...  can you please comment on this?  <br>Many thanks, Marcin <br></div>\r\n"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "zMzqoL0jDAAJ",
   "from": "Altuna Akalin",
   "author": "ab8277113f65c05e",
   "date": "2017-03-30T13:07:57Z",
   "subject": "Re: [methylkit_discussion] methylkit/eDMR",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, I can&#39;t comment much on eDMR as I&#39;m not maintaining that package and I didn&#39;t read that paper you mentioned. It depends what they used for testing the performance. The only thing that would convince me for DMR performance is using CTCF peak loss/gain between cell types and overlapping those with DMRs coming from replicated WGBS data. <div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Altuna</div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Thu, Mar 30, 2017 at 12:35 PM, Gmail Marcin <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">marcin.b...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, I was looking at Klein and Hebestreit paper 20016 where methylKit in eDMR mode was compared to other software where regional significance are of interest.  I see that methylkit does not perform that great when it comes to the sensitivity methylkit/eDMR  (Fig3). I wanted to use this mode for differential analysis of the regions.  I am not sure if the reason for it is that maybe overdisperssion was not implemented yet before running the diff tests? I also did not like their description about the methylkit/eDMR as what comes into the analysis is already diff object.  Heterogeneity of the samples is taken in test already ...  can you please comment on this?  <br>Many thanks, Marcin <br></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
