   "subject": "Re: [methylkit_discussion] methylkit/eDMR",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks for the comments Altuna and Chris. Indeed, sad that reviewer did not see it. It brings the doubts on methylKit for experienced users. Is anyone running differential analysis on predefined regions (not tiles) but rather CpGs, shores or gene promoters, first exons, second exon etc ...?   I would like to summaries the methylation values first on these regions before running differential methylation after, so genomics ranges will be different size for every widow.   Many thanks Marcin<br></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 30 March 2017 at 15:20, Christopher Mason <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">christoph...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">One other note is that the paper you mentioned applies far more filters to the other tools than methylKit/eDMR (Figure 2), which make the comparison between the tools a bit of apples to oranges.<div><a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/bib/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/bib/bbv095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://academic.oup.com/bib/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/bib/bbv095&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793232627596000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1oEczC1aLq3jphYiGDC-VV\">https://academic.oup.com/bib/article-lookup/doi/10.1093/bib/bbv095</a></div><div><br></div><div>Other are limited to 0.9 quantile, which would dramatically change the results, along with some of the other filters.</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
