     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><div><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div>On Mar 30, 2017, at 9:07 AM, Altuna Akalin &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">aak...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:</div><br></blockquote></div></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><div><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, I can&#39;t comment much on eDMR as I&#39;m not maintaining that package and I didn&#39;t read that paper you mentioned. It depends what they used for testing the performance. The only thing that would convince me for DMR performance is using CTCF peak loss/gain between cell types and overlapping those with DMRs coming from replicated WGBS data. <div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Altuna</div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
