     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div><div><div><div><div><blockquote type=\"cite\"><div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, I was looking at Klein and Hebestreit paper 20016 where methylKit in eDMR mode was compared to other software where regional significance are of interest.  I see that methylkit does not perform that great when it comes to the sensitivity methylkit/eDMR  (Fig3). I wanted to use this mode for differential analysis of the regions.  I am not sure if the reason for it is that maybe overdisperssion was not implemented yet before running the diff tests? I also did not like their description about the methylkit/eDMR as what comes into the analysis is already diff object.  Heterogeneity of the samples is taken in test already ...  can you please comment on this?  <br>Many thanks, Marcin <br></div></blockquote></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
